//? Title: Davis–Putnam: Actual Resolution, Clause Growth and Checked SAT Verdicts
//? Subtitle: Complete elimination traces, the original four formulas and independent truth-table controls
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A propositional formula in conjunctive normal form asks whether some Boolean
assignment satisfies every clause. Enumerating all assignments is expensive,
and removing a variable can create many new constraints. Davis–Putnam instead
eliminates variables using forced literals, pure literals and resolution.
We begin with a two-variable contradiction whose four parent pairs produce
two opposite unit clauses and two discarded tautologies. Complete native
traces expose the actual formulas and counters, retain the original four
examples, and demonstrate a constructed quadratic clause-growth step.
Independent truth tables check small inputs and the existential meaning of
every transition. Complete validation and explicit resource limits distinguish
an exact verdict from a request that exceeds available work or history.
)

\toc

> Four parent pairs, two unit clauses, one contradiction

Let x=x₁ and y=x₂. The conjunction of all four possible two-literal clauses
on these variables is false under every assignment. Eliminating x pairs each
positive parent with each negative parent. Two unions contain both y and its
negation and are always true, so they add no constraint. The other two are
the opposite units {y} and {¬y}. Propagating either unit empties the other.
The diagram and table below are built from the actual core history; no
solver stages are reconstructed by the report.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/davis_resolution.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,{{1,2},{1,-2},{-1,2},{-1,-2}});
  if(t.satisfiable || t.steps.size()!=2 || t.work.resolution_pairs!=4) return 1;
  std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<int>>> states{t.initial};
  for(const auto& s:t.steps) states.push_back(s.after);
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(75,1100);dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle box;box.width=3.5;box.height=3.1;box.font_size=21;box.fill={219,234,254};
  const std::vector<std::string> rules{"unit","pure","resolution"};
  std::print("| stage | actual signed literal clauses | rule just used | pivot/literal | pairs | tautologies discarded |\n| ---: | :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<states.size();++k) {
    std::string label;for(const auto& c:states[k]) label+=c.empty()?"empty clause\n":std::format("{}\n",c);
    if(k==2)box.fill={254,226,226};
    d.node(std::to_string(k),{5.4*k,0},label,box);
    if(k==0) std::print("| 0 | {} | initial | 0 | 0 | 0 |\n",states[k]);
    else {const auto& s=t.steps[k-1];const auto rule=rules[static_cast<std::size_t>(s.rule)];
      d.edge(std::to_string(k-1),std::to_string(k),std::format("{} {}",rule,s.literal));
      std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,states[k],rule,s.literal,s.pairs,s.tautologies);
    }
  }
  d.text({5.4,2.8},"Signed integers encode literals; each row is a disjunction",21)
   .text({5.4,-2.8},std::format("{} parent pairs; {} tautologies discarded; SAT = {}",t.work.resolution_pairs,t.work.tautological_resolvents,t.satisfiable),22)
   .save_svg("build/davis_resolution.svg");
}
```
