\caption(The three boxes contain the normalized input, the actual resolution result and the actual unit-propagation result. An empty clause is false; an empty collection of clauses would be true.)
\alttext(Four signed two-literal clauses become the units minus two and two after resolving variable one. Unit minus two then leaves an empty clause. Four parent pairs were visited and two tautologies discarded.)

> Derive what variable elimination preserves

For a variable x, partition the current CNF into clauses (x∨Aᵢ), clauses
(¬x∨Bⱼ), and an x-free remainder R. The Aᵢ and Bⱼ are disjunctions with the
pivot removed. Resolution computes

\[
\exists x\left[R\land\bigwedge_i(x\lor A_i)\land
\bigwedge_j(\neg x\lor B_j)\right]
\quad\equiv\quad R\land\bigwedge_{i,j}(A_i\lor B_j).
\]
\alttext(Existentially quantifying the pivot equals keeping the pivot-free remainder and every positive-negative parent resolvent.)

Fix the values of every other variable. If some Aᵢ is false, the left side
requires x=true; if some Bⱼ is false, it requires x=false. These demands
conflict exactly when one pair has both Aᵢ and Bⱼ false, which is exactly
when its resolvent is false. If neither conflicting demand occurs, choose x
accordingly. This proves both directions, including a missing polarity:
then no parent pairs exist and the pivot can satisfy all its clauses.
Tautological unions may be omitted because they are always true; repeated
literals and clauses may be deduplicated because disjunction and conjunction
are idempotent. No subsumption or hidden branching is used here.

A unit {ℓ} requires ℓ=true. Remove every satisfied clause and delete −ℓ from
the others. A pure literal has no opposite in the formula, so setting it true
cannot falsify another clause. Both rules likewise preserve the existential
projection after removing their variable. They do not preserve the original
formula with that variable left free: {x} projects to true, but is not true
for the assignment x=false. Repeating the argument proves the final SAT
verdict. Every step removes at least one occurring variable; checking the
empty clause first and empty set second terminates in UNSAT or SAT after at
most the number of occurring variables.

The API decides finite *propositional CNF satisfiability*. Davis and Putnam's
1960 paper embeds propositional reasoning in a first-order proof procedure;
it does not supply a terminating decision procedure for arbitrary first-order
validity. This report and the catalog entry use the narrower implemented
contract. The 1962 Davis–Logemann–Loveland procedure replaces eliminating
resolution with splitting and backtracking; the distinct DPLL API is used
below only as a comparison.

> Keep the original formulas and measure actual solver work

The original four formulas remain, including its five-clause, four-variable
3-CNF. That fixed example is labelled as fixed, rather than newly sampled
random data. Empty CNF, an empty clause and tautology/duplicate controls make
the edge conventions explicit. The plot measures actual resolution pairs;
a zero means the simplification rules sufficed, not that solving took no work.
Every result also agrees with the existing DPLL solver on these valid inputs.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/davis_putnam.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<int>>> formulas{
    {{1,2},{-1,3}},{{1},{-1}},{{1,2},{1,-2},{-1,2},{-1,-2}},
    {{1,-2,3},{-1,2,4},{2,-3,-4},{1,3,-4},{-1,-2,4}}, {}, {{}}, {{1,1},{1},{2,-2}}};
  const std::vector<int> variables{3,1,2,4,0,0,2};
  const std::vector<bool> expected{true,false,false,true,true,false,true};
  const std::vector<std::string> labels{"original 1","original 2","original 3","original 4","empty CNF","empty clause","duplicates"};
  std::vector<double> x,pairs;
  std::print("| case | SAT | unit steps | pure steps | resolution steps | parent pairs | peak clauses | DPLL agrees |\n| :--- | :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=0;k<formulas.size();++k) {
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(variables[k],formulas[k]);
    const bool agrees=t.satisfiable==dm::algorithms::dpll_sat(variables[k],formulas[k]).satisfiable;
    if(t.satisfiable!=expected[k] || !agrees) return 1;
    const auto& w=t.work;x.push_back(k);pairs.push_back(w.resolution_pairs);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",labels[k],t.satisfiable,w.unit_steps,w.pure_steps,w.resolution_steps,w.resolution_pairs,w.peak_clauses,agrees);
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(x,pairs,"parent pairs",{37,99,235},.6).legend({"parent pairs"},{{37,99,235}})
   .x_ticks(x,labels).title("Actual resolution work on original and boundary formulas")
   .x_label("fixed CNF case").y_label("positive-negative parent pairs visited")
   .x_limits(-.6,6.6).y_limits(0,6).size(1100,570).save_svg("build/davis_putnam.svg");
}
