> Construct a real clause-growth step

For order k≥2, use x, distinct a₁…a_k and b₁…b_k. The reusable core generator
constructs k clauses (x∨aᵢ), k clauses (¬x∨bⱼ), a clause containing all ¬aᵢ,
and a clause containing all ¬bⱼ. Every variable has both signs and no clause
is a unit, so the smallest-variable policy resolves x first. Each of its
k² parent pairs yields a different (aᵢ∨bⱼ), with no tautology or duplicate.
The two negative tails survive. Thus the *actual first completed state* has

\[
C_0=2k+2,\qquad P_1=k^2,\qquad C_1=k^2+2,\qquad
C_1-C_0=k(k-2).
\]
\alttext(Input clauses are two k plus two; first-step pairs are k squared; after clauses are k squared plus two; net growth is k times k minus two.)

The formula is always UNSAT: x=false forces all aᵢ true, contradicting the
negative a tail; x=true forces all bⱼ true, contradicting the negative b
tail. This is a resolution-growth control, not a pigeonhole encoding or a
random SAT benchmark. The program completes every solve and reports peak
clause count and cumulative pairs separately from the first-step quantities.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/davis_growth.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  std::vector<double> k_values,input,after,predicted;
  std::print("| order k | input clauses | first pairs | first after clauses | predicted after | peak clauses | total pairs | SAT |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | :--- |\n");
  for(std::size_t k=2;k<=8;++k) {
    const auto f=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_grid_formula(k);
    const auto t=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(static_cast<int>(2*k+1),f);
    const auto& s=t.steps.front();const auto theory=k*k+2;
    if(t.satisfiable || s.literal!=1 || s.rule!=dm::algorithms::DpRule::resolution || s.pairs!=k*k || s.after.size()!=theory || s.tautologies || s.duplicates) return 1;
    k_values.push_back(k);input.push_back(t.initial.size());after.push_back(s.after.size());predicted.push_back(theory);
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",k,t.initial.size(),s.pairs,s.after.size(),theory,t.work.peak_clauses,t.work.resolution_pairs,t.satisfiable);
  }
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.line(k_values,input,"input",{220,38,38},2).line(k_values,after,"actual after",{37,99,235},2)
   .points(k_values,predicted,"k squared + 2",{31,41,55},4)
   .legend({"input","actual after","k squared + 2"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235},{31,41,55}})
   .title("One real elimination: linear input, quadratic next state")
   .x_label("grid order k").y_label("distinct non-tautological clauses")
   .x_limits(1.5,8.5).y_limits(0,85).size(1000,580).save_svg("build/davis_growth.svg");
}
```
