\alttext(Input counts rise from six to eighteen as order grows from two to eight. Actual first-state counts rise from six to sixty-six, coinciding with k squared plus two.)

> Costs, normalization and complete limits

If a pivot occurs positively in p clauses and negatively in q clauses, its
resolution loop visits exactly pq parent pairs. At most pq new clauses are
inserted, but tautologies and duplicates can reduce that count. Ordered sets
canonicalize literals and clauses; there is no subsumption filter. Clause
scans, literal-set construction, union operations, tree comparisons,
allocation and copying are real additional costs and are excluded from the
pair counter. With C clauses and maximum width W, a conservative bound for
the pair-generation/insertion part is O(pq W log(W+1) log(C+pq+1)). Unit and
pure transitions also scan formulas. No measured pair curve represents
elapsed time or all operations.

On r remaining variables, at most 3^r non-tautological distinct clauses exist:
each variable is absent, positive or negative. The empty clause is included.
This is a finite upper bound, not a polynomial one. Decreasing the variable
count does not make the clause count decrease; the grid example shows why.
Live old and next formulas coexist during a transition. Retaining complete
history adds the sum of all snapshot sizes to memory. `peak_clauses` measures
only the largest completed-state clause count, not allocator bytes or the
combined live memory footprint.

`davis_putnam` keeps its original Boolean call shape and exact verdict for
valid inputs, while invalid variable bounds, zero literals, INT_MIN and
out-of-range ids now fail explicitly. Validation checks every input literal
before a trivial verdict or a resource limit, even inside tautologies or
beside an empty clause. A sparse bound such as INT_MAX creates no array of
that length. The trace's three optional caps bound distinct clauses in each
normalized set, cumulative resolution parent pairs, and cumulative snapshot
entries. A snapshot costs one entry plus one per clause plus one per literal;
even empty states cost an entry. Each next pair batch and snapshot copy is
checked in full. Exceeding a cap throws without returning a partial SAT/UNSAT
answer. Zero disables that optional cap, while arithmetic and capacity checks
remain. These caps do not bound input width, temporary unions, bytes or time.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/davis_contract.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <climits>
int main() {
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const std::vector<std::vector<int>> f{{1,2},{1,-2},{-1,2},{-1,-2}};
  const auto t=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,f,4,4,20);
  std::vector<std::vector<std::vector<int>>> states{t.initial};for(const auto& s:t.steps)states.push_back(s.after);
  std::vector<double> x,cost;std::size_t total=0;
  std::print("| snapshot | clauses | literals | charged entries | cumulative entries |\n| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n");
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<states.size();++i) {
    std::size_t literals=0;for(const auto& c:states[i])literals+=c.size();const auto entries=1+states[i].size()+literals;total+=entries;
    std::print("| {} | {} | {} | {} | {} |\n",i,states[i].size(),literals,entries,total);x.push_back(i);cost.push_back(entries);
  }
  if(total!=20) return 1;
  int rejected=0;
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,f,3,4,20);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,f,4,3,20);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,f,4,4,19);}catch(const std::length_error&){++rejected;}
  try{(void)dm::algorithms::davis_putnam(INT_MAX,{{},{INT_MIN}});}catch(const std::invalid_argument&){++rejected;}
  const auto normalized=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,{{1,1},{1},{2,-2}});
  const bool sparse=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam(INT_MAX,{{INT_MAX}});
  const bool disabled=dm::algorithms::davis_putnam_trace(2,f,0,0,0).satisfiable;
  if(rejected!=4 || !sparse || disabled || normalized.initial!=std::vector<std::vector<int>>{{1}})return 1;
  std::print("\nFour expected rejections: clause cap, pair cap, history cap, invalid INT_MIN despite empty clause. Count {}.\n",rejected);
  std::print("Normalized duplicate/tautology control: {}; SAT {}. Sparse INT_MAX unit: SAT {}. All optional caps disabled: SAT {}.\n",normalized.initial,normalized.satisfiable,sparse,disabled);
  auto p=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  p.bars(x,cost,"entries",{37,99,235},.6).legend({"entries"},{{37,99,235}})
   .x_ticks(x,{"initial","after resolution","after unit"}).title("Complete history: 13 + 5 + 2 = 20 charged entries")
   .x_label("actual stored snapshot").y_label("1 + clauses + literals")
   .x_limits(-.6,2.6).y_limits(0,16).size(1000,560).save_svg("build/davis_contract.svg");
}
```
