> Independent verification and scope

The native tests compare the solver with direct assignment enumeration for
all 512 subsets of the nine canonical two-variable clauses, all 3,304
three-variable formulas with at most three distinct canonical clauses, and
1,500 seeded formulas through seven variables. The oracle evaluates clauses
directly and does not implement another elimination recurrence. For every
traced step and every assignment in these controls, the tests compare the
new formula with the disjunction of the old formula under both values of the
removed variable. Separate checks reconstruct canonical resolvents, discarded
tautologies, duplicate conclusions and actual counters. Original tests also
retain their 500 seeded comparisons with DPLL. Eight new tests pass both
AddressSanitizer and UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer, including complete caps,
invalid-before-trivial-verdict precedence, sparse extremes, ownership and the
grid's exact first-state law. These are finite controls, not an independent
machine-checked proof or a performance comparison of general SAT solvers.

The report needs no external dataset: the tiny formulas expose the logical
rules, while the purpose-built family exposes a concrete growth mechanism.
The API returns a decision and an explanatory history, not a satisfying
assignment or a standalone refutation certificate. Canonical sorting fixes
which unit, pure literal and pivot is chosen; another ordering can have very
different intermediate size. The results establish the original verdicts,
the existential rule invariant, one exact quadratic growth step and explicit
failure semantics for bounded traces.

> References

- Martin Davis and Hilary Putnam (1960), *A Computing Procedure for Quantification Theory*, Journal of the ACM 7(3), 201–215. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/321033.321034); [paper PDF](https://web.stanford.edu/class/cs357/DP60.pdf). The implementation follows the finite propositional unit, pure and eliminating rules, adding canonical deduplication and explicit resource checks.
- Martin Davis, George Logemann and Donald Loveland (1962), *A machine program for theorem-proving*, Communications of the ACM 5(7), 394–397. [Original paper DOI](https://doi.org/10.1145/368273.368557). This is the distinct splitting/backtracking successor used only for the comparison API.
